rise-conditioning as the MECHANISM (the greater the height difference, the gentler the ramp must be, because the effort must be sustained longer). THREE SOURCES, THREE METHODS, ONE CONDITIONING STRUCTURE, and this is the first to measure the rise half rather than state it. THE OVER-READ TO GUARD AGAINST is quoting 1:8 as a permissive maximum: the same abstract reports NO SIGNIFICANT DIFFERENCE BETWEEN 1:10 AND 1:12 (as suggested by ADAAG), so the paper is not arguing for steeper ramps generally -- it is saying the code distinction is not detectable at low rise and that a steeper ramp is tolerable when the rise is small. extraction_method SKIM on a deposited abstract; the full text is not read and the participant count is not stated in it.', '2026-09-18 21:36', 'session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining', '2026-09-18 21:36', 'session_2026-09-18-research-batch-17-vredenburgh-full-read-and-mining', 'claim', '<=');
